Relevant columns and their possible roles  

mst_users  
• user_id : primary key to count each unique user once  
• birth_date : used to derive the user’s age (year‑of‑birth → age → age‑category)  
(Optional)  
• withdraw_date : if you want to exclude already‑withdrawn members (e.g. WHERE withdraw_date IS NULL)  

Typical conditions you may add  
• birth_date IS NOT NULL – ignore unknown birthdays  
• withdraw_date IS NULL – keep only active members (if required)  

Below are ten exploratory‐to‑final SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows where applicable).  
Every query is different and is preceded by a comment that explains what it does.

```sql
-- 1) Quick look at the first 20 user records
SELECT *
FROM "mst_users"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List distinct birth_date values that exist in the table
SELECT DISTINCT "birth_date"
FROM "mst_users"
WHERE "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Extract distinct birth‑year part only (YYYY)
SELECT DISTINCT substr("birth_date", 1, 4) AS "birth_year"
FROM "mst_users"
WHERE "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Count how many users we have for each birth‑year
SELECT substr("birth_date", 1, 4)  AS "birth_year",
       COUNT(*)                    AS "users"
FROM   "mst_users"
WHERE  "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY "birth_year"
ORDER  BY "birth_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Show user_id together with a rough age (today’s year minus birth_year)
SELECT "user_id",
       (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER)) AS "age"
FROM   "mst_users"
WHERE  "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) See distribution of users in 10‑year brackets (20s, 30s, 40s, 50s, others)
SELECT
  CASE
    WHEN age BETWEEN 20 AND 29 THEN '20s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 30 AND 39 THEN '30s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 40 AND 49 THEN '40s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 50 AND 59 THEN '50s'
    ELSE 'others'
  END AS "age_band",
  COUNT(*) AS "users"
FROM (
  SELECT
    (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
     CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER)) AS age
  FROM "mst_users"
  WHERE "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
)
GROUP BY "age_band"
ORDER BY "age_band";
```

```sql
-- 7) Same as #6 but restricted to users who have not withdrawn
SELECT
  CASE
    WHEN age BETWEEN 20 AND 29 THEN '20s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 30 AND 39 THEN '30s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 40 AND 49 THEN '40s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 50 AND 59 THEN '50s'
    ELSE 'others'
  END AS "age_band",
  COUNT(*) AS "active_users"
FROM (
  SELECT
    (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
     CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER)) AS age
  FROM "mst_users"
  WHERE "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
    AND "withdraw_date" IS NULL          -- keep only active members
)
GROUP BY "age_band"
ORDER BY "age_band";
```

```sql
-- 8) Check if any user falls below 20 years old (sanity check for 'others')
SELECT "user_id",
       (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER)) AS "age"
FROM   "mst_users"
WHERE  "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
  AND  (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER))  < 20
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Verify the 'others' band by picking users aged 60+
SELECT "user_id",
       (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER)) AS "age"
FROM   "mst_users"
WHERE  "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
  AND  (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
        CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER))  >= 60
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL answer: number of users in each requested age category
SELECT
  CASE
    WHEN age BETWEEN 20 AND 29 THEN '20s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 30 AND 39 THEN '30s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 40 AND 49 THEN '40s'
    WHEN age BETWEEN 50 AND 59 THEN '50s'
    ELSE 'others'
  END AS "age_band",
  COUNT(*) AS "users"
FROM (
  SELECT
    (CAST(strftime('%Y','now') AS INTEGER) -
     CAST(substr("birth_date",1,4) AS INTEGER)) AS age
  FROM "mst_users"
  WHERE "birth_date" IS NOT NULL
)
GROUP BY "age_band"
ORDER BY "age_band";
```
